Book principles and our [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) mappings are separate. Each entry names the book and the chapters or sections we read. Unknown edition details stay unknown. Quote numbers match the links on the durability pages.

## Books

### B1 Erlang and OTP in Action

Logan, Martin; Merritt, Eric; Carlsson, Richard. *Erlang and OTP in Action*.

**Read:**

- The Erlang/OTP Platform: Let It Crash
- process isolation
- §4.2.2 supervisor restart strategy

**Edition:** Edition, publisher, and year are not confirmed.

[Public book reference](https://www.manning.com/books/erlang-and-otp-in-action).

#### Q01

> The point of letting an entire subsystem terminate and be restarted is that it brings you back to a state known to function properly.

### B2 Elixir in Action

Jurić, Saša. *Elixir in Action*, Third Edition. Manning, 2024.

**Read:**

- §5.4.3 Shared-Nothing Concurrency
- Chapters 8–9, fault tolerance and supervision
- Other Use Cases for ETS

[Public book reference](https://www.manning.com/books/elixir-in-action-third-edition).

#### Q02

> Remember from chapters 8 and 9 that processes lose their state on termination.

#### Q03

> Shared-nothing concurrency ensures complete isolation between processes: one process can’t compromise the internal state of another.

### B3 Designing Elixir Systems with OTP

Gray II, James Edward; Tate, Bruce A. *Designing Elixir Systems with OTP*. Pragmatic Bookshelf, 2019.

**Read:**

- Build a Functional Core
- Isolate Process Machinery in a Boundary
- Customize Your Lifecycle
- Big, Loud Worker-Bees
- Add Persistence as a Boundary [Service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies)
- Test the Boundary

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://pragprog.com/titles/jgotp/designing-elixir-systems-with-otp/).

#### Q04

> Elixir can’t manage what it doesn’t know about.

#### Q14

> Our core doesn’t access external interfaces or use any process machinery your component might use.

### B4 Release It

Nygard, Michael T. *Release It!: Design and Deploy Production-Ready Software*. Pragmatic Bookshelf, 2007.

**Read:**

- §4.4 Unbounded Result Sets
- Chapter 5 Stability Patterns
- §5.1 Timeouts
- §5.2 [Circuit Breaker](/lore/circuit-breakers-have-state)
- §5.3 Bulkheads
- §5.4 Steady State
- §5.5 Fail Fast
- §5.6 Handshaking
- §5.7 Test Harness
- §5.8 Decoupling Middleware

**Edition:** Not stated. Do not substitute second-edition chapter numbering.

[Public book reference](https://archive.org/details/releaseitdesignd0000nyga).

#### Q05

> The timeout is a simple mechanism allowing you to stop waiting for an answer once you think it will not come.

#### Q06

> calls to the circuit breaker fail immediately, without any attempt to execute the real operation.

#### Q07

> Protect critical clients by giving them their own pool to call.

#### Q08

> The Steady State pattern says, for every mechanism that accumulates a resource, some other mechanism must recycle that resource.

#### Q09

> The Test Harness can produce slow responses, no responses, or garbage responses.

### B5 Building Event-Driven Microservices

Bellemare, Adam. *Building Event-Driven Microservices: Leveraging Organizational Data at Scale*. O’Reilly, 2020.

**Read:**

- Chapter 3 Communication and Data Contracts
- Chapter 6 Deterministic Stream Processing
- Chapter 7 Stateful Streaming
- Chapter 9 Microservices Using Function-as-a-Service
- Chapter 14 Supportive Tooling

**Edition:** Not stated. Quotes come from the 2020 text.

[Public book reference](https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/building-event-driven-microservices/9781492057888/).

#### Q11

> The overarching goal of deterministic processing is that a microservice should produce the same output whether it is processing in real time or catching up to the present time.

#### Q12

> Schema evolution ensures that the consumer can translate it to a familiar version.

#### Q13

> allowing a single stateful transaction to atomically update both the state and input offsets.

### B6 Fundamentals of Data Engineering

Reis, Joe; Housley, Matt. *Fundamentals of Data Engineering*. O’Reilly.

**Read:**

- Part II, Chapter 5: Message Queues
- DataOps considerations
- Incident Response

**Edition:** Edition and year are not confirmed.

[Public book reference](https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/fundamentals-of-data/9781098108298).

#### Q10

> In an idempotent system, the outcome of processing a message once is identical to the outcome of processing it multiple times.

### B7 Domain Modeling Made Functional

Wlaschin, Scott. *Domain Modeling Made Functional: Tackle Software Complexity with Domain-Driven Design and F#*. Pragmatic Bookshelf, 2018.

**Read:**

- Implementation: Working with Errors
- Using the Result Type to Make Errors Explicit
- Handling Exceptions
- Composing in Parallel with Applicatives

**Edition:** Printing P1.0. The numbered edition is not stated.

[Public book reference](https://pragprog.com/titles/swdddf/domain-modeling-made-functional/).

#### Q15

> These different kinds of errors require different implementations.

### B8 Monitoring Distributed Systems

Ewaschuk, Rob. *Monitoring Distributed Systems*. O’Reilly, 2016.

**Read:**

- The Four Golden Signals
- Black-Box Versus White-Box
- Symptoms Versus Causes

**Edition:** First edition. It is also a chapter of the Google SRE book.

[Public book reference](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/).

#### Q18

> The four golden signals of monitoring are latency, traffic, errors, and saturation.

### B9 Serverless Architectures on AWS

Sbarski, Peter; Cui, Yan; Nair, Ajay. *Serverless Architectures on AWS*, Second Edition.

**Read:**

- Chapter 6, The New Search Microservice: Everything Fails, All the Time
- Pay Attention to Retry Configurations

AWS-specific mechanisms are not universal runtime guarantees.

**Edition:** Publisher and year are not confirmed.

[Public book reference](https://www.manning.com/books/serverless-architectures-on-aws-second-edition).

#### Q20

> Do you put the failed event into a DLQ and process it later?

### B10 A Philosophy of Software Design

Ousterhout, John. *A Philosophy of Software Design*. 2018.

**Read:**

- §4.4 Deep Modules
- §10.2 Too Many Exceptions
- §10.3 Define Errors Out of Existence
- §10.6 Mask Exceptions
- §10.7 Exception Aggregation
- §10.8 Just Crash?
- §10.10 Taking It Too Far

**Edition:** Edition and publisher are not confirmed.

[Public book reference](https://web.stanford.edu/~ouster/cgi-bin/book.php).

#### Q16

> Rather than throwing an error when unset is asked to delete an unknown variable, it should have simply returned without doing anything.

### B11 Property-Based Testing

Hebert, Fred. *Property-Based Testing with PropEr, Erlang, and Elixir*. Pragmatic Bookshelf, 2019.

**Read:**

- Chapter 7 Shrinking
- Laying Out Stateful Properties
- Testing Parallel Executions
- Initial State for the State Machine
- Adjusting the Model

Use the actual breaker’s threshold semantics.

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://pragprog.com/titles/fhproper/property-based-testing-with-proper-erlang-and-elixir/).

#### Q17

> Both function calls must be deterministic.

### B12 Site Reliability Engineering

Beyer, Betsy; Jones, Chris; Petoff, Jennifer; Murphy, Niall Richard, editors. *Site Reliability Engineering: How Google Runs Production Systems*. O’Reilly, 2016.

**Read:**

- Chapter 3 Embracing Risk
- Chapter 22 Addressing Cascading Failures (Mike Ulrich)
- Chapter 26 General Principles of SRE as Applied to Data Integrity
- Chapter 27 Reliable Product Launches at Scale

Section-only excerpts limit detailed attribution.

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://sre.google/sre-book/table-of-contents/).

### B13 Grokking Simplicity

Normand, Eric. *Grokking Simplicity: Taming Complex Software with Functional Thinking*. Manning, 2021.

**Read:**

- Chapters 15–17: Isolating Timelines, Sharing Resources Between Timelines, Coordinating Timelines

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://www.manning.com/books/grokking-simplicity).

### B14 Refactoring Databases

Ambler, Scott W.; Sadalage, Pramod J. *Refactoring Databases: Evolutionary Database Design*. Addison-Wesley, 2006.

**Read:**

- §3.11 What You Have Learned (transition period)
- Chapter 4 Deploying into Production

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://www.amazon.com/Refactoring-Databases-Evolutionary-paperback-Addison-Wesley/dp/0321774515).

#### Q19

> many refactorings require you to run both the original and new versions of the schema in parallel during a transition period that is long enough to allow any applications accessing that portion of the schema time to be redeployed.

### B15 Software Engineering at Google

Winters, Titus; Manshreck, Tom; Wright, Hyrum, editors. *Software Engineering at Google: Lessons Learned from Programming Over Time*. O’Reilly, 2020.

**Read:**

- Chapter 15 Deprecation: Deprecation Tooling and Preventing Backsliding
- CI Is Alerting (hermetic and flaky tests)

**Edition:** Not stated.

[Public book reference](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book).

### B16 Why Programs Fail

Zeller, Andreas. *Why Programs Fail: A Guide to Systematic Debugging*. Morgan Kaufmann, 2009.

**Read:**

- Chapter 4 Reproducing the Problem Environment
- Chapter 10 Reference Runs

Short section excerpts do not establish a complete debugging method.

**Edition:** Not stated.

## Coverage limits

Some books gave no usable passage for this topic:

- *Designing Data-Intensive Applications* and Newman’s *Building Microservices* gave only fragments for their [scope](/lore/scopes-own-resources).
- *Adopting Elixir* and *Programming Elixir* gave no usable findings.
- The Scala text gave no usable passage.

These pages make no book-backed claims about fencing, consensus, compensation, or replication. They add no hot-upgrade or Governor rule.

## Effect source

Effect contributors. *Effect*, `effect@4.0.0`, commit `67ba4e46a11ccda0b6761578bfd22c04ae00167d`. See [the durability skill](/skills/durability) and its [reference copy of this bibliography](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/skills/durability/references/bibliography.md). All mappings and proposed checks are our own reading of the books.

